  First question, and it's the one that determines everything else.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how does the running page know [3mwhich[0m environment it's in? A browser has no env vars, so this is either
  decided at deploy time (a file is written/swapped before the page ships) or at page-load time (the code inspects
  something and picks).

  - [1mRuntime detection from hostname[0m — the settings module maps [38;5;153mlocation.hostname[39m to an endpoint. Buys: one artifact
    ships everywhere, zero build tooling, works today with a static file server. Costs: every environment's URL is
    visible in shipped source; adding an environment means a code edit. Rules out little — cheap to replace later.
  - [1mDeploy-time config file[0m — [38;5;153mconfig.js[39m (or a [38;5;153mwindow.APP_CONFIG[39m block) is generated or swapped per environment; the
    settings module just reads it. Buys: environments stay out of the source, standard 12-factor shape. Costs: you now
    need a deploy step that didn't exist, and local dev needs a default. Harder to undo — it puts a build/deploy
    contract in place.
  - [1mBuild-time bundling[0m — introduce a bundler, [38;5;153mimport[39m the settings module, inline the value per build. Buys: real module
    system for the whole app, dead-code elimination. Costs: a toolchain, [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m, and a build for a two-file
    static page. This is the big one to walk back.

  [1mMy recommendation: runtime detection from hostname.[0m The app is a static page with a stubbed login that never actually
  calls the endpoint. Hostname mapping gets you environment switching with no toolchain and no deploy contract, and if
  you later add a bundler or a deploy step, the settings module's [3minterface[0m ([38;5;153msettings.apiEndpoint[39m) doesn't change — only
  its internals do. The other two buy infrastructure this repo isn't paying for yet.
